[Music]  hey you guys welcome back to tombs of  terror  and we're doing another book review  today just like we always do on this  show now the one we're doing today this  is a little bit more of a novella really  that's kind of short this one is called  knight  of the mannequins by steven graham jones  this actually came out in 2020  and i guess this was another one that  wasn't on my tbr list  but it just kept coming up in my  uh recommendations like on amazon and i  kept seeing like other  book channels covering it and uh  that and his other book which i think  they came out the same year which was  called  the only good indians which i've also  heard a lot of really good stuff about  but because this one was so short and  this is the first time that i'm reading  anything by stephen graham jones i think  i might have read some of his short  stories because his name is very  familiar  but so i wanted something that was kind  of like short to see  if i wanted to kind of go whole hog and  read some of his other stuff  even though from everything that i've  heard this one is not quite like  some of the other stuff that he's  written but  i don't know i liked this one enough  that i would definitely like read some  more stuff  of his now the thing about this book is  that it's so  short like i said i think it's only  about 130 pages you can easily read it  in one sitting  it's so short that it's gonna be really  really difficult for me to like talk  about without  spoiling it and honestly when i was  reading the back of the you know the  blurb on the back  it kind of spoils one of the things  anyway  so i really didn't think it was that big  a deal so like i said if you want to  read this  just like usual if you don't want to  know anything about it which as i said  that's the way i like to approach horror  novels i don't really even read the  blurbs or anything  i just like see somebody recommended it  and gives a sentence or two synopsis and  i'm just like okay that sounds good so  and then i just like start reading it  but if you don't want to know anything  anything about it then  go read it it probably will only take  you an hour or two and then come back  and we can talk about it and you know  we'll see what you thought about it we  can talk about it in the comments if you  would like to  so this book is basically it's from it's  in the first person  and we're hearing it in the voice of a  teenage boy  named sawyer so keep in mind if you're  not real into the style the writing  style of this book  it's very much as though you're  listening to  a teenage boy talk you know what i mean  it's almost kind of like  i saw another review that compared it to  like somebody just  you know even like making a youtube  video or something like that where he's  like confessing all of this stuff that  happened so it's very conversational  um you know very off the cuff and um so  if  so if you think it took me a little bit  like a few pages to get  into the writing style you know what i  mean just because it was so  it was so much like a teenager would  talk but after that it didn't really  bother me anymore  so keep in mind that if you don't really  like that type of  you know conversational type of tone  then maybe this knot  might not be uh your thing but because  that's how the whole thing is written  so you would think from the cover and  from  the title it almost kind of seems like  it seems like steven graham jones was  trying to i don't know if i'd  necessarily say he's trying to like  subvert  like b movie tropes because the way that  it was kind of marketed the way that it  sounds  on the back of the book almost makes it  sound like  a slasher which it kind of is  or like one of those b movie like big  monster type of  things or like you know or a town gets  overrun by living mannequins like  tourist trap or something like that  that's  the first thing i thought it was tourist  trap actually which you should watch if  you haven't seen it it's actually really  good it's from the 70s but the book is  not really like that at all as i said  it's sort of like  a slasher but it's kind of a  psychological  thing as well with like some ambiguity  to it  so what you have is that there's this  small town and there's this group of  friends  uh boys and girls and they're all  getting ready to i guess this is going  to be their last summer  um you know after they graduate high  school or or whatever so they're  probably  they're you know they're probably going  to be separating so  you have this group they've been friends  for a really long time now this one  particular summer before the events of  the book  they found an old abandoned department  store mannequin like in a creek or  something in the woods  and for a long time they would kind of  just wag this mannequin around  and play pranks on the other townsfolk  with it that was like something that  they were  really fond of doing but this last  summer um i guess  at this point as i said everyone's  thinking about going off to college or  moving away  and so they're not really thinking about  the mannequin anymore and the mannequin  who is named manny  very uncreatively has kind of been  forgotten about so he's kind of just in  sawyer's garage like sitting on his  dad's old motorcycle or whatever  but sawyer decides that because it's  their this is their last summer together  he wants to pull one last prank  kind of bring the mannequin out of  retirement and pull this one last  prank on uh particularly his friend  shauna who has just gotten  a job at a at the local movie theater  but also  on kind of like the the manager of the  movie theater because he kicked some of  the boys out for  you know sneaking in without paying and  they kind of wanted to get back at him  for that so what they decide to do is  dress manny up like a person uh you know  like you would  and put him in the movie theater and  then just kind of have him sit there and  then they're gonna sit in the back  and you know and watch the hilarity  ensue like when  the manager comes around looking for  tickets or when shauna comes around  and uh you know sees that it's a  mannequin and not a real person and they  think it's going to freak him out and so  they think that's really funny  now what ends up happening so they do  this i love like all the stuff about  where he's trying to  they're trying to sneak the mannequin  into the movie theater which  you know obviously that's kind of hard  to do and they have to sort of cut it  into pieces  you know what i mean like take it apart  cut it into pieces and like put it in  their backpacks and whatnot  and then they have to sort of reassemble  it like in the front row  so they do all that they put clothes on  it and they put it in there so  uh the prank seems to be going  swimmingly uh the usher comes around  to look at this person's ticket  but nothing happens you know as i think  they bought it a ticket but then if they  like put it in his pocket or something  and it looked like the the guy that was  going around checking the tickets like  looked at the lap and they just kept on  going like nothing was weird  and they were like well huh that's  really unusual and they thought that  their prank was kind of  you know gonna go over like a wet fart  or whatever and then  something really weird happens after the  movie is over  the mannequin apparently gets up and  walks out  like a regular person so from this point  forward sawyer becomes convinced  that manny the mannequin has come to  life  and is essentially punishing his friends  for forgetting about him because as i  said they used to take him everywhere  and pull all kinds of pranks with him  so he's kind of like given it agency and  given it life  in his head now not too long after this  their friend shauna the girl who works  at the movie theater  is killed along with some of her family  members in this really horrific like  freak accident like a truck like drives  through the front of their house or  whatever even the dogs get killed  and they're all just like turned into  complete mush  and because of this freak accident he  becomes convinced that manny the  mannequin  is going around killing each of his  friends one by one  and also killing their families like in  revenge  and he even has these other ideas that  manny is going around because i guess  there's kind of a rash in the  neighborhood of  people's um kind of sheds and stuff  being broken into and then  the only thing stolen is you know bags  of fertilizer so he gets the idea that  manny is eating fertilizer and that he's  growing up to like kaiju size  like so he's like a godzilla vatican so  the more the story goes on the more  the scenario becomes more complicated in  his mind  and to him it's logical that because  shauna and her whole entire family were  wiped out  presumably by manny the mannequin he  thinks that  by killing all his friends himself  that he will spare the family members  because to him he figures well you know  it's bad that you know four or five  teenagers have to die but  you know if i don't do this then  manny will wipe out all of their  families and that's even more people so  he thinks he's being a hero so there's  like a thread through there  because there's a thread too about the  for the movie that they see  at the beginning where they first do the  prank it's like some superhero  which is supposed to be like a marvel  movie but he doesn't call it that but  that's what he's referring to  and then it seems like every subsequent  event that's like significant to the  story  has something to do with that movie like  you know shawna was like downloading it  when the truck crashed into the house  you know there's a scene at a drive-in  where it's also playing  so it's so it kind of becomes wrapped up  in his mind that that movie that he's  being a superhero  in a way that he's doing something he  doesn't really want to do or something  difficult for a greater good that's kind  of like what his  thinking is so as i said  the thing about this is that  sawyer pretty much from the outset and  like i said this  this is a spoiler review um so don't  complain i told you that at the  beginning  but sawyer is essentially an unreliable  narrator obviously  or i guess they kind of leave it in  biggest theater i'm not really sure but  um or obviously manny  is not has not grown to godzilla size  and is like wiping all these people out  this is sawyer just making up this story  in his head and using these weird events  happening in the town to sort of feed  into this narrative that he's  constructed  to give him justification for killing  all his friends  presumably although this isn't stated  outright but this is what i got from it  anyway  because he was upset that all of his  friends were going to be  leaving him so i think that's why he  sort of projected those feelings onto  manny like you know we forgot about him  in the garage  and you know he now he wants to kill us  all because  you know we used to hang out he was our  best friend and  you know now everyone's leaving and  forgot about him so i kind of feel like  those were his feelings but he was  projecting that onto the mannequin  because as the story goes on  you know he starts killing like picking  off his friends he starts wearing like a  mannequin  mask uh you know almost like he's you  know doing the work of the mannequin  because he thinks that  if he doesn't do it then the mannequin  is going to kill  more people um i saw this one review  earlier and this was actually a pretty  astute observation i can't remember what  her name was but she was reviewing this  book as well  and she said this actually kind of gave  her the  vibe of like remember that case where  those two little girls like  thought that they had to kill their  friend for slenderman otherwise  slenderman was going to wipe out their  whole families  it's kind of like that but as i said  this is all from  sawyer's point of view even though you  can say oh it's not  all that believable that someone would  come to believe that a mannequin had  come to life  and had grown to giant's eyes and was  going to wipe out i mean  the fact that that it did happen in real  life in the slenderman case albeit  you know those kids were much younger  than this boy is supposed to be supposed  to be  17 or 18 years old but he's obviously uh  got mental  issues so the fact that he just develops  this  really complicated scenario about this  mannequin  just you know what i mean so it is  unbelievable but  in a way not you know what i'm saying i  actually really really enjoyed this book  like i said it  take it took me a couple pages to get  into the groove  of the writing style because as i said  it's very casual it's very off the cuff  very conversational  and it's just like a teenager would talk  so you have to kind of get  you have to kind of have to vibe with it  and it takes a couple pages to do that  um it's kind of short so you don't  really get to know  any of the other characters all that  well um you're basically just seeing  them through  sawyer's perception because he's the one  talking about it also at the end i will  say that  i i think i want to say that they left  it ambiguous that manny wasn't real  um you know that he wasn't really  walking around but in a way they kind of  don't  i mean stephen graham jones i don't know  he doesn't really explain  well then why did sawyer because i think  i remember from the beginning  that it wasn't just sawyer that saw  manny ostensibly  get up and walk out of the theater so it  doesn't really explain  why they saw that or what happened to  the mannequin subsequently  unless he just kind of imagined that  whole scenario i'm not really sure so  they do kind of leave that a little bit  vague  there was kind of a little bit of a  thing at the end where one character you  thought was dead is still alive  um you know so but i didn't really mind  that like some people didn't really like  that  plot twist or whatever but i didn't mind  it but yeah i just this is just a really  fun it's like a slasher and it's funny  but it's also kind of like a little bit  grim and a little bit like kind of  disturbing but  i don't know it's hard to explain it's  just it's just like a really funny  slasher but it has like a lot of  psychological  uh kind of [ __ ] to it too and it's like  you know if you like unreliable  narrators  if you kind of like you know just like a  fun quick  i don't want to say it's kind of a  breezy read you know it's really fun  like i said it's not maybe not what  you're expecting it's not like a  army of mannequins like coming down the  town or anything like that it's not like  that at all it's basically just like  this one  kid and his you know spiraling psychosis  as he  like imagines that this mannequin is  killing these people but  it's still fun and it's still funny it's  like a got a really dark sense of humor  and um it's kind of it's pretty gruesome  it had like some of the kills were  pretty gruesome and stuff like that so i  really uh enjoyed it  and it didn't really take me all that  long to read so if you're looking to get  into stephen graham jones because as i  said he's written several books um  the best his uh recent one was called  the only good indians which like i said  i want to read that too because that's  gotten like a really lot of good reviews  but it's significantly longer than this  one but yeah  so it's called uh night of the  mannequins and um  pretty cheap pretty short so if you  are in the mood for something like that  then uh by all means check it out  and i will see you on the next one bye

